KNOWNBUG
always_latch1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always \(main\.en \|-> main\.q == main\.d\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
always_latch (IEEE 1800-2017 9.2.2.3) denotes a level-sensitive latch: the
latched variable follows its data input while the enable is active and holds
its previous value otherwise. The design latches q = d while en is high, and
the property observes q only while en is high, so q == d must hold and p0
should be PROVED.

Observed: EBMC rejects the construct with
"always_latch is not supported by RTL construction" and reports a CONVERSION
ERROR (exit code 2) instead of proving the property.

Root cause: verilog_rtl_buildert::build_always in src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp
has no case for ID_verilog_always_latch and throws unconditionally. This has
never been supported (the earlier synthesis module removed in commit a445c7fd
likewise rejected always_latch with "no synthesis support for always_latch"),
so it is a long-standing missing feature rather than a regression; a maintainer
may prefer to file it under FUTURE. Semantically always_latch could be built
like always_comb (WIRE for variables assigned on all paths) but with
unassigned paths retaining the previous value, i.e. a level-sensitive state
element.
